    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "gotcha. so basically we're saying we should just strip all that away so it sounds like standard english. i'm just curious tho—when we're training the staff, does that specific way of talking (the \"i be\" and so on) actually make it harder to take the customer seriously in a corporate context? like, if a manager reads a report with those patterns, would they see it as a clear, serious issue, or does that style of speech make the person sound less educated or less capable of expressing a legitimate problem? i just want to make sure i'm framing the \"why\" for the team properly.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When documented using highly informal speech patterns, reports can unintentionally dilute the perceived seriousness of the issue to a manager. Corporate documentation generally favors standard English because it conveys immediate professionalism and clarity of thought. By editing to standard grammar, you signal that the *problem* is being treated seriously, regardless of the customer's original vernacular. This helps your team focus their internal language on solutions rather than the customer's communication style.",
      "source": "target",
